ief. In the first sense, we aim to believe what is most probably true according to our total evidence, including testimony from peers and authorities. In the second, we aim to base our beliefs only on objective evidence and argumentation, even if that lowers the probability of their being true. The first principle of rational belief tends to serve the short-term epistemic interests of individuals, while the second tends to serve the long-term epistemic interests of both individuals and groups. The best way to reconcile these principles in cases of peer disagreement is to associate them with two corresponding species of belief, here called perception and opinion."}, {"id": "https://openalex.org/W2336005475", "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199226078.003.0007", "title": "Peer Disagreement and Higher‐Order Evidence", "publication_year": 2010, "type": "book-chapter", "cited_by_count": 580, "open_access": {"is_oa": true, "oa_status": "green", "oa_url": "https://philpapers.org/archive/KELPDA.pdf", "any_repository_has_fulltext": true}, "primary_location": {"id": "doi:10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199226078.003.0007", "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199226078.003.0007", "is_oa": false, "landing_page_url": "https://doi.org/10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199226078.003.0007", "pdf_url": null, "source": {"id": "https://openalex.org/S4306463708", "display_name": "Oxford University Press eBooks", "issn_l": null, "issn": null, "is_oa": false, "is_in_doaj": false, "is_core": false, "listed_in": [], "host_organization": "https://openalex.org/P4310311648", "host_organization_name": "Oxford University Press", "host_organization_lineage": ["https://openalex.org/P4310311648", "https://openalex.org/P4310311647"], "host_organization_lineage_names": ["Oxford University Press", "University of Oxford"], "type": "ebook platform"}, "license": null, "license_id": null, "version": "publishedVersion", "is_accepted": true, "is_published": true, "raw_source_name": "Disagreement", "raw_type": "book-chapter"}, "abstract": "This chapter distinguishes and assesses a set of views about the rational response to peer disagreement. It raises a series of objections to views according to which one should assign ‘equal weight’ to one's own view and the views of those with which one disagrees. It concludes by defending ‘The Total Evidence View’, according to which what one is justified in believing in cases of peer disagreement depends upon what is supported by one's total evidence."}]", "excerpt_truncated": true, "source_sha256": "bb5da3a7bad52f77aa6bee223fcf916f3bf1e915bc21b93bdd05e6eebf36b03e", "verification_required": true, "topic_domain": "judgment_and_consensus", "evidence_role": "discovery", "host_tier": "discovery", "source_identity": null, "persistent_identifiers": ["doi:10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199226078.003.0007", "openalex:W2621159184", "openalex:W2336005475"], "source_candidates": ["https://irl.umsl.edu/thesis/212", "https://doi.org/10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199226078.003.0007"], "source_candidate_identities": {}}

